# Workflow files stay LF in every checkout.
#
# They are read by Linux runners, and by guards that split them on a bare line
# feed. On 2026-09-16 three separate guards reported false failures in agent
# worktrees, where the files had been checked out with CRLF: one sweep found
# zero run commands in nine workflows and reported success over code it had not
# read, and one job-slicer never found the job boundary, so it read the *next*
# job's secrets and reported a secret in a job that holds none. Three agents
# spent time on it. The files are LF in the index either way; this makes the
# working tree agree.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yaml text eol=lf

# Firestore rules are hashed LF-normalised by the preservation register and
# deployed as text; a CRLF working copy makes the hash disagree with itself.
*.rules text eol=lf

# The reference corpus is held together by hashes: every case source is checked
# against the `Quellenhash:` its case declares, and `manifest.json` carries the
# SHA-256 of the case book it was built from. All three are computed over the
# LF form, so a CRLF working copy would make the corpus disagree with itself in
# exactly the way the workflow files did above.
# The appendix carries its own `MANIFEST.sha256.json` over its own files, so it
# gets the same treatment rather than a CRLF copy that fails its own verifier.
docs/korpus/** text eol=lf
tests/korpus/** text eol=lf

# An archived Terms version is evidence of a wording: `lib/terms-versions.ts`
# carries the SHA-256 of each file and a consent record stores it. The digest is
# taken over the LF form, so a CRLF checkout would still hash correctly — this
# keeps one from appearing in the first place, which is also what makes the file
# a reader opens byte-for-byte the file the digest names.
docs/terms/** text eol=lf

# The process benchmark is frozen by hashes: `frozen-*.json` carries the SHA-256
# of every case's `expected.json` and source, taken over the LF form, and
# `validate.py` compares the working copy against them. A CRLF checkout made all
# 300 cases report drift (1,992 errors) while nothing had changed. Same cure as
# the corpus above; the cases themselves are never edited.
tests/prozess-benchmark/** text eol=lf

# The ABAP file format schemas are copied byte for byte from SAP/abap-file-formats
# (MIT) and tests/sap-references.spec.ts compares each one with the SHA-256 the sync
# recorded. No line-ending conversion in either direction, so the copy stays the
# upstream file.
lib/generated/aff-schemas/** -text

# tests/security-audit-guard.spec.ts holds the security agent's mail to the app's
# mail shell character for character: it reads the RESPONSIVE_STYLES template out
# of lib/email-layout.ts as text and compares it with the value the .mjs copy
# evaluates to. A template literal's value never carries a CR (ECMAScript
# normalises CRLF to LF in template text), so a CRLF working copy of the source
# made the two differ on every line while the content was identical (2026-10-13).
lib/email-layout.ts text eol=lf
scripts/security/lib/mail-shell.mjs text eol=lf
